@section('content')
  <h1>{{ $title }}</h1>
  <p>The new and edit pages draw a form from the resource's <code>fields</code>. When the form is submitted, the resource's <code>validate</code> method, or <code>validate_update</code> on an edit, turns the values into its input, or returns a message for each field.</p>

  <h2>Field kinds</h2>
  <p>Each field has a kind, which sets the input and how the value is read:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><code>Text</code>, a single-line text input.</li>
    <li><code>Textarea</code>, a multi-line text input.</li>
    <li><code>Password</code>, a password input. The form never fills it in, not even on edit.</li>
    <li><code>Number</code>, a number input. The value is read as text, and the resource parses it.</li>
    <li><code>Checkbox</code>, a tick box. A tick sends <code>on</code>, and an empty box sends nothing, so the value is false.</li>
    <li><code>Select</code>, a list of <code>(value, label)</code> choices.</li>
    <li><code>Date</code>, a date input. The value is read as <code>YYYY-MM-DD</code>.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>A hand-written resource can use every kind. The derive can use all of them except <code>Select</code>. A derived password field is a <code>String</code>, and it is hashed on save. See <a href="/docs/resources#passwords">Resources</a>.</p>
  <p>The panel does not check a <code>Select</code> value against its choices. The resource's <code>validate</code> must do that.</p>

  <h2>Required fields</h2>
  <p>A field marked <code>required</code> shows an asterisk after its label. The asterisk is only a hint: the resource's <code>validate</code> must reject a blank value. The derive does this for you, and a hand-written resource calls <code>check_required</code>, which trims the value and records the message <code>&lt;label&gt; is required.</code> if it is blank.</p>

  <h2>Validation errors</h2>
  <p>When <code>validate</code> returns errors, the panel shows the form again with the values the user typed, a message under each field that has one, and the status 422. Nothing is written to the database. A derived field that cannot be read, such as a number that is not a whole number, gets its own message, such as <code>Price must be a whole number.</code></p>
  <p>A value that clashes with a unique index is also a field error. A second user with the same email, for example, shows <code>Email is already taken.</code> under the email field, with the status 422. Any other database error is a 500.</p>

  <h2>Passwords</h2>
  <p>A password field is never filled in on the edit page. On create, a password is required. On edit, a blank password keeps the stored hash, and a new one is hashed with argon2 on save. A hand-written resource hashes with <code>ignite_core::hash_password</code> itself, and overrides <code>validate_update</code> to allow a blank password on edit. See <a href="/docs/resources#passwords">Resources</a>.</p>

  <h2>Forms and tokens</h2>
  <p>Every panel form carries a hidden <code>_token</code> field, which the panel checks on submit. A form that was open too long shows the Page Expired page, with status 419, and nothing is saved. See <a href="/docs/panel">Panel</a>.</p>

  <h2>Not built yet</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Help text and file fields.</strong> A field has a label and a kind. There is no help text, and no upload.</li>
  </ul>
